      "id": 1,
      "text": "Yorktown Heights Railroad Station, built in 1905 as a stop on the New York and Putnam Railroad Line, closed its doors in 1958.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yorktown_Heights%2C_New_York",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The former station earned a place on the National Register of Historic Places in 1981.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yorktown_Heights%2C_New_York",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Yorktown Heights takes its name from the 1781 Revolutionary War victory at Yorktown, Virginia, honored when the town incorporated in 1788.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        9,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Yorktown_Heights,_New_York",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Near the station, the Pines Bridge Monument honors the 1781 Battle of Pine’s Bridge, fought just south of present‑day Yorktown Heights.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yorktown_Heights%2C_New_York",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "European settlers first arrived in 1683, establishing farms along the Croton River within the vast Van Cortlandt Manor.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Yorktown_Heights,_New_York",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Nestled 45 miles north of New York City, Yorktown Heights rolls through wooded hills and open farmland.",
